                                             extracted by EDTA, NaOH, HNO3, and FA-Cd pools. The mobility of Cd in
                                             soils cultivated with cacao, based on plant uptake, was strongly associated with
                                             the soils’ chemical characteristics, especially pH and SOC. The surface
                                             application of organic matter facilitates the redistribution of Cd in soil
                                             fractions, mainly in EDTA-, NaOH-, and HNO3-extractable fractions,
                                             suggesting a reduction in Cd soil–plant transfer via adsorption or
                                             complexation processes. Apparently, the application of high-quality
                                             compost, i.e., high FA content, could aid in mitigating Cd contamination in
                                             cacao orchards. Experiments on perennial crops merit a longer evaluation time
                                             to better assess the changes in plant-Cd.




