    Determination of Heavy Metals in Locally Available Chocolates in Lahore Region
    Muhammad Amjad1,a,*, Shabbir Hussain1,b, Zia Ur Rehman Baloch2,c, Aoun Raza1,d
    1
      Department of Chemistry, Lahore Garrison University, Lahore-Pakistan
    2
      Department of Pharmacy University of Sargodha, Sargodha-Pakistan
    *
      Corresponding author
    ARTICLE INFO                        ABSTRACT
                                        Heavy metal toxicity can either be acute or chronic effects. Long-term exposure of the body to
    Research Article                    heavy metal can progressively lead to muscular, physical and neurological degenerative process. In
                                        this research, a total of 30 representative chocolate samples were collected from local shops and
                                        markets in Lahore. All the samples were analysed to assess the levels of Lead (Pb), Nickel (Ni),
    Received : 20/02/2021               Chromium (Cr) and Cadmium (Cd) by using Atomic Absorption Spectrophotometer in Pakistan
    Accepted : 14/03/2021               Council of Scientific and Industrial Research (PCSIR), Lahore. It was observed that the majority of
                                        chocolate samples contain heavy metals levels higher than the permissible limits as recommended
                                        by Punjab Food Rules (PFR) 2011. The levels of Pb ranged between 0.375 and 3.4 mg/kg with 90%
                                        samples having concentrations exceeding PFR allowable limit (0.5 mg/kg). Whereas, Ni
    Keywords:                           concentrations varied from 0.005 to 0.28 mg/kg and 80% samples were above the PFR limit (0.025
    Heavy metals                        mg/kg). Further, Cr levels analysed between 0.005 to 0.28 mg/kg and 53% samples had Cr
    Toxicity                            concentrations higher than PFR limit (0.02 mg/kg). Similarly, Cd levels were observed between
    Chocolate                           0.50-3.25 mg/kg with 53% samples having Cd concentrations exceeding the PFR limit (1.0 mg/kg).
    Lahore                              Investigation indicates that the quality of available chocolates is not recommendable for eating
    Atomic Absorption                   because of the higher concentrations of toxic heavy metals.


